From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: FullPanel takes the strip again after a display change

What: in "Full screen including notch" (FullPanel), a full screen that a
display change left below the notch covers the strip again once the
displays settle, without leaving full screen. Three parts:
- A frame change is sorted by omacvm_fp_frame_kind: a frame laid out for
  another display than NSScreen still names (not inside it), or on a
  display CoreGraphics has off or asleep, is a display change on its way
  ("display change: ...; the strip comes back once the displays settle"),
  not a lost strip. A strip taken as lost that went with a display change
  within 3 s (the window's display changed, or the one it covered went off)
  is turned into one. Split View's loss stays as before.
- The claim on the strip (the reservation answer) no longer waits for
  -screenChanged:'s resume after a display without a camera housing:
  AppKit may lay the window out on the notched display before
  NSWindowDidChangeScreen comes. AppKit's own yes already means the Space's
  display has a housing.
- Once the displays settle (the display update ran, unlocked, awake, on the
  console, nothing due), once the displays' windows are in full screen, and
  when the user comes back to the VM's Space, a full screen below the menu
  bar's band after a display change asks AppKit once to lay its tile out
  again: -[_NSFullScreenSpace updateTileShapeAndReshapeContents] on
  -[[window _fullScreenContentController] space], which asks the
  reservation again. Only after AppKit's own
  -alwaysReservesSpaceForMenuBarInFullScreen says the Space's display has a
  housing, on the active Space, visible, with WindowServer's frame in
  place; never Split View; at most 3 tries per full screen, one per settle.
  Every class, selector and type encoding is checked, each the class's own
  method with AppKit's code; main thread, @try. No frame is set, nothing is
  toggled, no Space is touched. The return check engages: "strip taken
  again after a display change (try N of 3)", "strip covered", and the
  guest's size and arrangement again. Not in 2 s: "strip not back after a
  display change (AppKit kept the band, try N of 3): normal full screen
  ..." (omacvm check warns, with the remedy).

Why: the user's MacBook Pro M4 Max (OmacVM 3.2.9, 2026-10-28), FullPanel on
the built-in with an LG UltraFine 5K: after unplugging, replugging and
sleep the strip beside the notch stayed away until full screen was entered
again. When the built-in left the display set, AppKit laid the main window
out for the LG while NSScreen still named the built-in (0,-323 2560x1440
on 0,0 1728x1117); the "display unchanged" check passed, so it was taken
as "strip lost" (meant for Split View), which blocked the resume, the
return checks and the reservation answer for the rest of that full
screen. The main window is never made again (unlike the other displays'
windows), so when its Space came back to the built-in AppKit kept the menu
bar's band (1728x1084) for good. Even without that, the strip came back
only if AppKit laid the window out once more after NSWindowDidChangeScreen;
nothing asked it to.

Fallbacks: a missing or changed AppKit part (another macOS) gives today's
behaviour with one line ("strip not back ... (selector missing: ...)");
leaving and entering full screen again still covers the strip. With
"Always show the menu bar in full screen" and Keep the Dock away off, no
try ("menu bar always shown").

Upstream: not applicable (private AppKit, OmacVM's notch strip).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-fullpanel-retake.sh (the rules, the
user's log in every order, and the wiring in the patched ui/cocoa.m at
build time: the retake only from its three triggers, nothing in it that
moves a window, a Space or the menu bar, the selectors checked by name,
types and image); the strip itself only on a notched Mac
(src/tests/e2e/display-unplug.sh, case fullpanel-builtin). Applies after
omacvm-cocoa-display-settle.patch.

